  "worked_solution": "We need to express the summation $S(k+1)$ in terms of $S(k).$\n\nLet ${a}_{i}$ for $i\\ge 2$ be a sequence, and let $S(k)$ denote the sum of the first $k-1$ terms.\n\n$$\nS(k)={\\sum }_{i=2}^{k}{a}_{i}\n$$\n\nWe can always express $S(k+1)$ in terms of $S(k)$ by writing the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=2}^{k+1}{a}_{i}=({\\sum }_{i=2}^{k}{a}_{i})+{a}_{k+1}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${a}_{i}=i(i-1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1}=(k+1)(k+1-1)=k(k+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=2}^{k+1}i(i-1)=({\\sum }_{i=2}^{k}i(i-1))+(k(k+1)),\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as \n\n$$\nS(k+1)=S(k)+k(k+1).\n$$",
